  "narrative": {
    "intro": "The federal government obligated $635B in contracts in FY2026 to date. The Department of Defense obligated $332B. The Department of Veterans Affairs obligated $74.2B. The Department of Homeland Security obligated $62.5B. The Department of Energy obligated $51.3B. The General Services Administration obligated $23.4B. The Department of Health and Human Services obligated $18.6B. The National Aeronautics and Space Administration obligated $13B. The Department of Transportation obligated $11.3B. The Department of Agriculture obligated $8.7B. The Department of Justice obligated $8B. The top ten agencies by obligations show concentration in defense and large civilian procurement agencies. The ranking lists obligations through the fiscal year to date and does not represent a full fiscal year total. Contractors and planners should treat these figures as partial-year obligations when comparing agency procurement activity across fiscal years. The list orders agencies by reported obligations for FY2026 to date and highlights where the largest contracting dollars have been recorded so far this fiscal year.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies account for the largest contract obligations?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense obligated $332B. The Department of Veterans Affairs obligated $74.2B and the Department of Homeland Security obligated $62.5B. The Department of Energy obligated $51.3B and the General Services Administration obligated $23.4B. The remaining top-ten agencies each obligated $18.6B or less."
      },
      {
        "heading": "How concentrated are contract obligations among the top agencies?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense accounts for the largest single share of obligations at $332B. The next two largest agencies, the Department of Veterans Affairs and the Department of Homeland Security, obligated $74.2B and $62.5B respectively. Agencies five through ten range from $23.4B down to $8B, reflecting a steep drop after the largest civilian agencies."
      }
    ]
  },
